WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.310
It's great to welcome to the program today.

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:09.140
Rutger Bregman, who is a historian and also
author of Humankind A Hopeful History and

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.549
the Bestselling Utopia for Realist.

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:15.320
You may also recognize him from such viral
moments as that time.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:18.330
He said the thing about taxes at Davos.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:22.700
And when Tucker Carlson flipped out on him
and tried not to air it.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:23.860
Rucker, great to have you on.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:25.170
I appreciate it.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:26.470
Thanks for having me, David.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:30.090
So to begin with, a hopeful history, humankind.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:35.850
I'm interested in this because particularly
right now, there are many people who who maybe

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:40.739
don't have the most optimistic perspective
on humanity and end on Homo sapiens.

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:47.100
So what is your source of general optimism
about humanity?

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.839
The book is really about a sort of silent
revolution that has taken place in science

00:00:52.839 --> 00:00:56.089
within, I'd say, the last 15 to 20 years.

00:00:56.089 --> 00:01:00.440
So what has happened is that scientists from
very diverse disciplines, anthropologists,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:07.390
sociologists, psychologists, you name it,
have moved from maybe a little bit cynical

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:12.090
view of human nature to a much more positive,
hopeful view of human nature.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:16.220
So in the book, I'm just sort of describing
this whole process and trying to point this

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:20.220
out, because what I discovered is that often
these scientists don't even know it from each

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.220
other.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:22.220
Right.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:26.420
There was one psychologist who at one point
during my research said to me when I told

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:30.920
her about, you know, stuff that is happening
in biology, she said, oh, my God, it's happening

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.110
there as well.

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:36.840
So I just wanted to to let people know that
this has been going on and it may be a source

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.500
for hope as well.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:44.060
How do you make the case that human beings
are sort of fundamentally good to the extent

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.350
that that's kind of definable, actionable
term?

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:52.510
Well, I wouldn't say that people are fundamentally
good, right, where we're clearly not angels.

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:53.510
Yes.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.110
What I would say is that what you assume in
other people is often what you get out of

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:58.110
them.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:01:59.110
Right.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:03.080
So if you assume that most people are selfish
and they'll start designing your institutions

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:07.860
around that idea, you know, your schools,
your marketplaces, everything you're doing

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.509
in society, then that's what you get out of
people.

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:11.509
Right.

00:02:11.509 --> 00:02:12.509
So what you assume is what you get.

00:02:12.509 --> 00:02:17.909
I think this is actually the defining idea
of what we call neo liberalism.

00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:18.909
Right.

00:02:18.909 --> 00:02:22.659
Which is basically people are selfish and
deal with it and design everything around

00:02:22.659 --> 00:02:24.379
it.

00:02:24.379 --> 00:02:28.239
I think it has sort of produced the kind of
people that the theory presuppose.

00:02:28.239 --> 00:02:30.390
And I think we can turn it around.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:37.530
And in the book, I try to give a lot of examples
of, for example, schools or or even organizations

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:43.970
or even prisons that work with this sort of
different kind of view of human nature and

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.420
then get some pretty interesting results as
well.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.409
Well, that's what's so interesting about it,
which is, as you point out, if if you take

00:02:50.409 --> 00:02:57.480
an economic perspective, if you believe people
are fundamentally greedy, then design an economic

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:58.599
system around that.

00:02:58.599 --> 00:03:02.920
And it has major implications that are difficult
to undo even if people aren't fundamentally

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:03.920
greedy.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:10.069
Yeah, if you look at evolutionary psychology,
if you look at social, etc., it every one

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:14.750
of the systems we've designed is based around
some of these core ideas.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:20.499
So the implications are actually not just
theoretical and trivial.

00:03:20.499 --> 00:03:24.389
The very real it's quite revolutionary idea.

00:03:24.389 --> 00:03:28.560
So for first it may sound like, oh, this guy
has written some nice happy clappy book about

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.030
how human beings are friendly.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:35.680
No, actually the implications are really revolutionary
because, you know, if we cannot trust each

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.459
other, then we need them right to keep us
in check.

00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:44.459
Then we need the CEOs and the managers and
the bankers and the monarchs and the generals

00:03:44.459 --> 00:03:45.459
and you name it.

00:03:45.459 --> 00:03:46.459
Then we need those in power.

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:48.030
Then we need hierarchy.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:52.739
If actually most people are pretty decent
and can be trusted.

00:03:52.739 --> 00:03:55.180
Then we can move to a radically different
society.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:59.309
You know, that is much more egalitarian where
people have the freedom to make their own

00:03:59.309 --> 00:04:00.309
choices.

00:04:00.309 --> 00:04:02.459
Suddenly, all kinds of things become possible.

00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:05.689
That initially were not possible at all.

00:04:05.689 --> 00:04:12.909
At what sort of level of focus do you think
is the right one to make these observations

00:04:12.909 --> 00:04:14.079
about humanity?

00:04:14.079 --> 00:04:16.349
And I'll give you an example of what I mean.

00:04:16.349 --> 00:04:25.229
You could take, for example, the Kitty Genovese,
the story of 1964 and the bystander effect

00:04:25.229 --> 00:04:28.639
and use that to extrapolate about human nature.

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:30.990
Or you could say, hey, you know what?

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:34.120
Over time, war has become less and less common.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.120
Right.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.990
These are very different levels of focus,
which may get you to different conclusions

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:40.550
about human nature.

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:45.160
Where do you think we should start evaluating
what human nature is?

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:48.830
Well, I think you need to look all these things
in the book.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:53.580
I start with a very old theory with a Western
culture, which is the idea of.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.030
Mere theory.

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:56.030
Right.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:58.069
And the idea goes like this.

00:04:58.069 --> 00:05:00.699
Supposedly civilization is only a thin veneer.

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:05.990
And as soon as something happens, say, a war
or a natural disaster or you ship wreck on

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:09.460
an island, then supposedly people reveal their
true selves.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:13.199
And, you know, they show that they're really
animals and they're selfish.

00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:16.169
If you look, for example, the news coverage
of natural disasters.

00:05:16.169 --> 00:05:17.449
It's pretty much always like this.

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:19.060
It's always veneer theory.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:23.930
You know, the news is full of stories about
looting and violence and plunder, you know,

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:25.449
the hawk and the horrible stories.

00:05:25.449 --> 00:05:30.500
But actually, what sociologists have known
basically since the 1960s is that what you

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:36.250
get every single time after a natural disaster
is an explosion of cooperation.

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:37.250
People really working together.

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:41.050
A great example is what happened after Katrina
in the US.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:45.699
Rebecca Solon has written a fantastic book
about this, A Paradise Built Now.

00:05:45.699 --> 00:05:48.199
But there are many other examples around the
globe.

00:05:48.199 --> 00:05:51.580
So it's really the opposite of Viniar theory.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:55.409
But still, this theory is so deeply entrenched
in our culture.

00:05:55.409 --> 00:05:57.280
You know, the idea that deep down we're all
selfish.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:59.530
It goes back all the way to the Asian Greeks.

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:01.850
You find it with the Christian church fathers.

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:03.880
You know, the idea that we're all born as
sinners.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:05.949
Even the Enlightenment philosophers.

00:06:05.949 --> 00:06:13.140
David, you Adam Smith, I think you could argue
that the US itself was sort of founded on

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:14.780
the idea that people are selfish.

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:19.199
If you read some of the texts of the founding
fathers, John Adams, for example, he wrote

00:06:19.199 --> 00:06:23.910
an essay that said all men would be tyrants
if they could, or James Madison, who said,

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:26.580
you know, ambition must be made to counteract
ambition.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.550
You know, let's let's be real.

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:30.730
Let's assume that most people are selfish.

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:34.780
So often when people say, oh, you've got to
be a realist, they say, well, don't be too

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:35.780
idealistic.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:36.780
Right.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:37.780
Otherwise, you're naive.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:43.110
And so I think that the larger project here
is sort of to try and redefine what it means

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:44.310
to be a realist.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:49.650
Is that actually the realistic scientific
position these days is to sort of conclude

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:54.219
that actually human beings have evolved to
cooperate?

00:06:54.219 --> 00:07:01.139
And there's actually this process in our evolutionary
history that biologists call survival the

00:07:01.139 --> 00:07:06.259
friendliest, which is very much the opposite
of what I had always thought.

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:12.500
What role in this analysis does the idea of
a sort of multi-layer evolutionary selection

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:20.719
have to play the idea that an individual being
greedy within a group that cooperates may

00:07:20.719 --> 00:07:27.460
may be a more relevant way to look than simply
humans are individually greedy or not greedy?

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:32.030
Well, there certainly are different theories
right now within biology, right, whether you

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:37.310
should look at evolution on the individual
level or on the group level.

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:39.639
And there's a lot of scientific debate going
on there.

00:07:39.639 --> 00:07:43.900
And to be honest, I don't really have a big
prefere position here.

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:49.650
I think sort of the more important takeaway
of, you know, developments within biology

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:54.060
of the past couple of years, say 15 to 20
years.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:59.710
Is that actually biologists have discovered
this process of what they call survival of

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:00.710
the friendliest.

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:01.710
Right.

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:05.900
So for thousands of years, it was actually
the friendliest among us who had the most

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:06.900
kids.

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:09.639
You know, imagine Donald Trump in pre-history.

00:08:09.639 --> 00:08:10.639
It's an impossibility.

00:08:10.639 --> 00:08:12.400
You know, he wouldn't have survived for long.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:16.510
People would have liked him, wouldn't want
to cooperate with him and would have expelled

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:18.689
him from the group and probably would have
died alone.

00:08:18.689 --> 00:08:19.689
Right.

00:08:19.689 --> 00:08:22.540
Or if he was really nasty, he would have been
executed by the group.

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:28.660
So we know this from studies from nomadic
hunter gatherer tribes around the globe, that

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:33.919
being friendly, being humble was really a
prerequisite if you wanted to survive.

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:39.380
Now, it's only recently in this area that
we called certain of the civilized era that

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:40.680
everything has changed.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:46.329
And nowadays, you know, there seems to be
this process that we call survival to shameless.

00:08:46.329 --> 00:08:47.329
Right.

00:08:47.329 --> 00:08:52.130
Or so often, if you are shameless, that can
actually be an advantage, which is, I think,

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:54.110
highly a natural for us as a species.

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:58.190
Because, you know, one of the most fascinating
things that I discovered during my research

00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:03.370
is that human beings are actually the only
species in the animal kingdom that blush,

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:07.160
you know, which is a fascinating fact that
we blush.

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:12.540
And for thousands of years, it was an evolutionary
advantage for us because it helped us to cooperate.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:16.550
And I think we need to go back to that vision
of human nature.

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:21.100
So I want to ask about what your vision of
human nature would imply.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:22.790
Maybe the economic system should be.

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:28.760
But before we get to that, the Viniar theory,
is it too early to see if or how that applies

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:31.320
to the world reaction to Corona virus?

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:35.649
Or do you have some sense of if and how that
applies to how we're currently seeing the

00:09:35.649 --> 00:09:37.230
world react to that pandemic?

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:38.510
Mm hmm.

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:43.240
Well, obviously, at first there were some
stories or quite a lot of stories in the press

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:47.740
about people hoarding toilet paper and, you
know, even fighting over toilet paper.

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:51.250
And again, it was very tempting to conclude
that, oh, here we have a crisis.

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:53.040
People are selfish once again.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:58.860
I think by now we can zoom out and say that
actually the vast majority of most people

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:01.780
has actually been pro-social in nature.

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:03.750
Most people are actually helping each other.

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:08.290
There's quite some evidence accumulating now
from different countries is that, you know,

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:11.449
it mostly brings out the positive, the good.

00:10:11.449 --> 00:10:16.380
And people you know, there is so many initiatives
from the bottom up, you know, groups on WhatsApp,

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:20.220
on Facebook and neighbors helping each other,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:24.560
And this is all not very surprising to sociologists,
because, as I said, you know, they've known

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:30.199
for a very long time that most people put
together during a time of crisis.

00:10:30.199 --> 00:10:33.779
It's not it's not what the Hollywood movies
have told you.

00:10:33.779 --> 00:10:37.560
You know, it's not like panic and everyone
going nuts and crazy.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:39.990
Flobots very much the opposite.

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:44.180
You know, one of the most striking eyewitness
accounts that I discovered during my research

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:48.560
was, for example, how some people behaved
during 9/11, you know, when they were going

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:53.029
down the stairs of the Twin Tower, Twin Towers
as they were burning.

00:10:53.029 --> 00:10:55.430
And people were literally saying, no, you
go first.

00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:56.500
Now you go for it.

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:59.800
Now this is crazy, but it actually happened.

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:07.160
So you talked about how seeing human nature
as fundamentally greedy implies or maybe justifies

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:13.610
the neoliberal economic framework, which is
very interesting if someone were to accept

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:15.829
your fundamental view of human nature.

00:11:15.829 --> 00:11:19.839
What is the economic system that would logically
follow from that?

00:11:19.839 --> 00:11:20.839
Hmm.

00:11:20.839 --> 00:11:22.750
Well, you could do a lot of things.

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:29.779
Actually, I started writing this book because
I had first written a book about the idea

00:11:29.779 --> 00:11:32.410
of giving everyone a guaranteed basic income.

00:11:32.410 --> 00:11:33.410
Right.

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:35.160
So this was utopia free of this.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:38.910
And the book was full of studies and examples
and experiments that showed that this could

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:40.490
actually work.

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:43.170
People in general have sort of two objections
against the idea.

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:47.019
And they say, well, it doesn't work because,
you know, people are lazy and they'll stop

00:11:47.019 --> 00:11:51.009
working or they'll say you can't do it because
people will waste the money.

00:11:51.009 --> 00:11:53.040
You know, they'll just spend it on drugs or
alcohol.

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:55.019
They're like the two most common objections.

00:11:55.019 --> 00:11:59.519
And so I sort of tried to collect all this
evidence and say, you know, it actually does

00:11:59.519 --> 00:12:00.519
work.

00:12:00.519 --> 00:12:01.519
We have tried it.

00:12:01.519 --> 00:12:03.899
And the the science says it works.

00:12:03.899 --> 00:12:09.320
So I went on a book tour and a couple of countries
and again and again and again, I found myself

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:14.880
discussing human nature with people because
Selma so often people would say, yeah, OK,

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:19.100
maybe this works on a local level, maybe this
works in this particular experiment here or

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:21.940
there in Canada or in the US in the 70s.

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:26.040
But that was a very different place, highly
exceptional.

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:29.960
In the end, you just got to look at human
nature and be realistic.

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:35.410
So I started to understand that for so many
of the exciting ideas that I cared about and

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:39.240
not only basic income, but also, for example,
different ways to do democracy, you know,

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:41.060
participatory democracy.

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:45.959
They all presuppose a different view, a more
hopeful view of human nature.

00:12:45.959 --> 00:12:49.329
And then I realized that actually I didn't
have that view myself.

00:12:49.329 --> 00:12:51.950
You know, I studied history.

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:57.240
I had, you know, studied so many of these
sort of cynical experiments from the 60s,

00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:01.510
the Stanford Prison Experiment or the Milgram
experiment that many people will know with

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:04.389
a shock machine.

00:13:04.389 --> 00:13:08.769
So I started basically on a journey with basically
to see if I if I.

00:13:08.769 --> 00:13:13.430
Could actually realistically adopt a more
hopeful view of human nature.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:15.810
And that resulted in this book.

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:20.550
And is there a specific I mean, from the point
of view of the vast majority of so-called

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:27.990
Western democracies, or is there a low hanging
fruit or so-called specific policies that

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:32.779
would be the ones that most obviously could
be changed for the betterment of society on

00:13:32.779 --> 00:13:36.240
the basis of your newfound optimism?

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:42.050
Well, maybe it's interesting to zoom in on
prisons, which is one of the most radical

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:46.050
applications of this theory of human nature.

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:50.860
If you go to Norway, you'll find prisons that
are really, really weird and strange where

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:54.259
they really treat inmates as if they're actually
people.

00:13:54.259 --> 00:14:00.070
So they're prisons like Bus Stoya or Hilborn,
very close to Oslo, where you'll find, you

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:05.139
know, people have been convicted of murders
or rapes or pretty horrible things, but still

00:14:05.139 --> 00:14:08.920
get the freedom, you know, to read books.

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:11.550
There there's a cinema, there's even a music
studio.

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:15.380
They have their own music label that is called
Criminal Records.

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:20.190
And and you would you know, some people would
say, well, this is like some kind of holiday

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:22.040
resort.

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:25.860
But then you look at the science behind it.

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:26.860
Right.

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:31.380
You look at the statistics and in particular,
you look at the recidivism rate and you realize

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:35.160
that actually Norway has the lowest recidivism
rate in the world.

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:39.610
So the chance that someone will commit another
crime, you know, after he or she gets out

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:44.100
of prison, that's the recidivism rate is nowhere
as low as in Norway.

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:47.769
So these prisons work even though they don't
really look like prisons, they're highly,

00:14:47.769 --> 00:14:49.040
highly effective.

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:53.930
Then you look at prisons in the US and you
realize that actually these prisons have have

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:56.700
the highest recidivism rate in the world.

00:14:56.700 --> 00:14:57.700
Right.

00:14:57.700 --> 00:15:00.230
If you treat people like crap, they'll will
be crap.

00:15:00.230 --> 00:15:03.630
I think I can't make it easier than that.

00:15:03.630 --> 00:15:07.910
If you treat people like people, they will
behave like people.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:11.290
This in the case of prisons, it really goes
against your intuition, right?

00:15:11.290 --> 00:15:15.579
You really sort of the intuition says vengeance.

00:15:15.579 --> 00:15:17.730
You know, people have done these horrible
things.

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:18.730
You want vengeance.

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:19.730
You want to lock them up.

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:21.949
You want them to suffer.

00:15:21.949 --> 00:15:27.889
But if you realistically look at what works,
then I think Norway really leads the way here.

00:15:27.889 --> 00:15:28.889
Yeah.

00:15:28.889 --> 00:15:32.940
It's also counterintuitive when one might
imagine you want prison to be really bad.

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:36.040
So people are more incentivized to avoid going
back.

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:40.129
And it just does not seem to actually function
function in that way, obviously.

00:15:40.129 --> 00:15:44.829
And the other problem is that prisons can
become sort of universities for crime.

00:15:44.829 --> 00:15:47.310
So this is really the case in the US people.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:51.600
People come in maybe for small minor offenses,
drug offenses or something like that, and

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:54.790
then they become hardened criminals in the
prison system.

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:55.790
Right.

00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:59.639
I for the book, I looked at the history of
the of of the system in the US.

00:15:59.639 --> 00:16:07.380
And it's really astonishing to find out that
actually in the early 70s, you know, the US

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:12.550
experimented with Norway style prisons, you
know, and it worked really well.

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:15.910
It's just that after that, history took a
different direction.

00:16:15.910 --> 00:16:17.019
Same is true for basic income.

00:16:17.019 --> 00:16:22.240
By the way, the U.S. almost implemented a
guaranteed basic income under Nixon.

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:26.470
You know, there's no country that came as
close to implementing a basic income as the

00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:30.170
U.S., but that didn't happen in the end.

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:33.139
No, it did because of a lot of bizarre coincidences.

00:16:33.139 --> 00:16:37.430
Yeah, even I mean, between World War 1 and
2, there was a moment where the direction

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:40.889
